  user_input {
    user_response: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Mouse Aiming Overshoot Reduction\n\n## Confirmed Problem\n\nThere is currently NO target-lock system.\n\nThe only problem being addressed is manual mouse aiming.\n\nThe user reports severe aiming overshoot:\n\n- Small mouse movements cause too much ship rotation.\n- The reticle frequently passes over the intended enemy position.\n- Counter-corrections cause another overshoot.\n- It is difficult to stabilize the reticle on a moving enemy.\n\nThis is a flight-control precision problem.\n\n## Objective\n\nImprove fine aiming precision without sacrificing fast directional turning.\n\nTarget style: Traditional Arcade Space Combat.\n\n## 1. Inspect the Current Controller\n\nTrace the actual relationship between:\n\n- Mouse movement.\n- Reticle displacement.\n- Steering demand.\n- Angular velocity.\n- Ship orientation.\n- Camera orientation.\n\nDetermine whether overshoot comes from excessive center sensitivity, excessive angular acceleration, accumulated input, smoothing behavior, or camera response.\n\nDo not guess.\n\n## 2. Prioritize Fine-Aiming Precision\n\nThe center region must support very small, controlled corrections.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Reduce excessive response to small mouse movements.\n- Avoid abrupt response changes.\n- Make small corrections predictable.\n- Avoid oscillation during repeated left/right corrections.\n- Ensure that stopping mouse movement stops adding steering demand.\n- Avoid unwanted angular momentum.\n\n## 3. Preserve Large-Turn Capability\n\nDo not solve the problem by simply lowering global mouse sensitivity.\n\nUse an appropriate nonlinear response curve so that:\n\n- Small inputs produce precise corrections.\n- Medium inputs produce progressively stronger turning.\n- Large inputs retain strong turning capability.\n\nThe transition must remain continuous and predictable.\n\n## 4. Strict Preservation\n\nDo not modify:\n\n- Pointer Lock implementation.\n- `movementX` / `movementY` input handling.\n- Physical mouse-edge continuity fix.\n- Aim boundary dimensions or shape.\n- Reticle reset behavior.\n- HUD layout.\n- Camera architecture.\n- Combat or weapon systems.\n\nDo not introduce a virtual joystick redesign.\n\nDo not add a target-lock system.\n\n## 5. Validation\n\nTest:\n\n1. Tiny left/right corrections.\n2. Repeated small corrections around a fixed point.\n3. Slowly tracking a moving enemy.\n4. Stopping the reticle on an enemy.\n5. Rapidly reversing steering direction.\n6. Large directional turns.\n\nMeasure overshoot and settling behavior where possible.\n\nActual browser testing with a real mouse is required before claiming the handling is fixed.\n\nIf browser testing is unavailable, report that limitation clearly.\n\n## Final Report\n\nExplain:\n\n- Root cause of overshoot.\n- Exact parameters or functions changed.\n- Why the change improves fine aiming.\n- Whether large-turn capability was preserved.\n- Actual browser test results.\n\nKeep the change small and independently reversible."
    items {
      text: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Mouse Aiming Overshoot Reduction\n\n## Confirmed Problem\n\nThere is currently NO target-lock system.\n\nThe only problem being addressed is manual mouse aiming.\n\nThe user reports severe aiming overshoot:\n\n- Small mouse movements cause too much ship rotation.\n- The reticle frequently passes over the intended enemy position.\n- Counter-corrections cause another overshoot.\n- It is difficult to stabilize the reticle on a moving enemy.\n\nThis is a flight-control precision problem.\n\n## Objective\n\nImprove fine aiming precision without sacrificing fast directional turning.\n\nTarget style: Traditional Arcade Space Combat.\n\n## 1. Inspect the Current Controller\n\nTrace the actual relationship between:\n\n- Mouse movement.\n- Reticle displacement.\n- Steering demand.\n- Angular velocity.\n- Ship orientation.\n- Camera orientation.\n\nDetermine whether overshoot comes from excessive center sensitivity, excessive angular acceleration, accumulated input, smoothing behavior, or camera response.\n\nDo not guess.\n\n## 2. Prioritize Fine-Aiming Precision\n\nThe center region must support very small, controlled corrections.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Reduce excessive response to small mouse movements.\n- Avoid abrupt response changes.\n- Make small corrections predictable.\n- Avoid oscillation during repeated left/right corrections.\n- Ensure that stopping mouse movement stops adding steering demand.\n- Avoid unwanted angular momentum.\n\n## 3. Preserve Large-Turn Capability\n\nDo not solve the problem by simply lowering global mouse sensitivity.\n\nUse an appropriate nonlinear response curve so that:\n\n- Small inputs produce precise corrections.\n- Medium inputs produce progressively stronger turning.\n- Large inputs retain strong turning capability.\n\nThe transition must remain continuous and predictable.\n\n## 4. Strict Preservation\n\nDo not modify:\n\n- Pointer Lock implementation.\n- `movementX` / `movementY` input handling.\n- Physical mouse-edge continuity fix.\n- Aim boundary dimensions or shape.\n- Reticle reset behavior.\n- HUD layout.\n- Camera architecture.\n- Combat or weapon systems.\n\nDo not introduce a virtual joystick redesign.\n\nDo not add a target-lock system.\n\n## 5. Validation\n\nTest:\n\n1. Tiny left/right corrections.\n2. Repeated small corrections around a fixed point.\n3. Slowly tracking a moving enemy.\n4. Stopping the reticle on an enemy.\n5. Rapidly reversing steering direction.\n6. Large directional turns.\n\nMeasure overshoot and settling behavior where possible.\n\nActual browser testing with a real mouse is required before claiming the handling is fixed.\n\nIf browser testing is unavailable, report that limitation clearly.\n\n## Final Report\n\nExplain:\n\n- Root cause of overshoot.\n- Exact parameters or functions changed.\n- Why the change improves fine aiming.\n- Whether large-turn capability was preserved.\n- Actual browser test results.\n\nKeep the change small and independently reversible."
    }
  }
}
