  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing TypeScript Errors**\n\nI\'m currently focused on pinpointing the root causes of the eight TypeScript errors flagged in `InputManager.ts` and `GameScene.tsx`. My immediate goal is to understand the nature of each error and establish a solid understanding of how those files work. I plan to trace the mouse aiming architecture for this usecase.\n\n\n**Investigating Config Mismatches**\n\nI\'ve traced the errors in `InputManager.ts` and `GameScene.tsx` to potential configuration discrepancies. It seems `steering.ts` may have been updated for `DIRECT_MOUSE_STEERING_CONFIG`, but `InputManager.ts` still references older parameters like `STEERING_CONFIG.stickSelfCenterRate`, leading to undefined properties and runtime exceptions. Specifically, the error is likely due to undefined parameters being passed to `input.consumeSnapshot()`. The `VirtualStickParams` in `InputManager` are also optional.\n\n\n**Tracing Mouse Overshoot**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the mouse aiming overshoot issue, as requested. I\'m focusing on the mathematical pipeline. My inspection will trace the relationship between mouse movement, reticle displacement, steering demand, angular velocity, ship orientation, and camera orientation. I\'m carefully examining `FlightController.ts`, `MouseResponseCurve.ts`, `steering.ts`, `AimBoundary.ts`, and `CameraController.ts` to identify the overshoot\'s source. I\'ll focus on the \'direct-nonlinear\' steering mode, analyzing reticle/gimbal behavior, response curves, angular rates, and camera behavior. Specifically, I see now that `steering.ts` may be missing parameters.\n\n\n**Calculating Mouse Input**\n\nI\'ve been calculating the precise numbers for small and micro mouse inputs based on the parameters in `DIRECT_MOUSE_STEERING_CONFIG`. I\'m focusing on the effect of the `inputNormalizationRange`, `mouseSensitivity`, and `responseCurve` on `u`. I\'m also examining the math in `evaluateDirectMouseSteering` to see the actual steering rates. The instantaneous mouse speed is computed as `velDt = Math.max(dt, 1 / 240)` which is a key component to look at.\n\n\n**Evaluating Response Curves**\n\nI\'m now evaluating the impact of the current response curve on steering. Specifically, I\'m examining how the formula `g(u) = a * u + (1 - a) * u^p` with `a = 0.25`, `p = 1.85`, and `maxYawRate = 3.5 rad/s` affects the yaw rate at different mouse input levels. It seems `maxYawRate` is 67% higher than the Interceptor\'s yaw speed, potentially causing over-sensitivity at low speeds, even with the blend and power curve.\n\n\n**Assessing Angular Rates**\n\nI\'ve been evaluating yaw rates at various mouse input levels. For u=0.25, the yaw rate is 24.1 deg/s, and for u=0.40, it\'s 47.7 deg/s. That may be only part 1! I\'m now examining `FlightController.ts` which has additional factors. Specifically, I see that lines 333-348 and 467-494 of `FlightController.ts` reveal a major problem with double-counting and reticle lag oscillation, due to `getAimDirectionWorld()`. The gimbal limits are also small!\n\n\n**Tracing Aiming Calculations**\n\nI\'m now investigating the computation of `boresightScreenX` and `boresightScreenY` in `CombatManager.ts`. It traces back to `flight.getAimTargetPoint`, which calls `flight.getAimDirectionWorld`. This method appears to compute the aim direction based on clamped gimbal angles. I will investigate how these calculations work, and how the gimbal parameters are defined. I will keep an eye on lines 470-494 and 333 of `FlightController.ts`.\n\n\n**Analyzing Reticle Deflection**\n\nI\'m now analyzing the implications of the reticle deflection logic, especially what happens when the player stops moving the mouse. My focus is on understanding the interaction between the ship\'s rotation, the reticle\'s deflection via `aimCursorX`, and the eventual aiming direction. I\'m investigating how this additional deflection impacts accuracy when the player intends to stop aiming at a target.\n\n\n**Examining Reticle Behavior**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the reticle behavior when mouse input ceases. Specifically, the reticle components, `boresightRef` and `aimReticleRef` in `FlightHUD.tsx`, are key. I see that the gimbal offset, calculated using `aimCursorX * 0.22 rad`, springs back to center over ~150ms. This causes the reticle to reverse its position, and jump ahead of the ship\'s nose, up to 12.6 degrees!\n\n\n**Investigating Velocity Integration**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a critical issue: the integration of mouse velocity.  The key problem I\'ve found lies in `FlightController.ts`. Specifically, the reticle\'s screen position, `aimScreenX`, is directly tied to a smoothed version of instantaneous mouse velocity (`normTurnX`). That\'s where I can solve the issue. This creates a visually jarring overshoot effect.\n\n\n**Refining the Velocity Issue**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a critical issue. The reticle\'s screen position, `aimScreenX`, is directly tied to a smoothed version of instantaneous mouse velocity, causing a jarring overshoot effect. The key problem I\'ve found lies in `FlightController.ts`. Specifically, the reticle\'s screen position is directly tied to a smoothed version of instantaneous mouse velocity. The gimbal\'s lead is `12.6\302\260` on top of the ship\'s rotation!\n\n\n**Evaluating Velocity Calculation**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how the velocity issue impacts aiming. Specifically, `aimScreenX` is directly tied to the smoothed mouse velocity, causing overshoot. I\'m seeing this is a visual issue, with the lead angle and resulting overshoot.\n\n\n**Fixing `consumeSnapshot()`**\n\nI\'m now focused on the errors in `InputManager.ts`, specifically the `TypeError` related to `consumeSnapshot()`. It appears missing fields in the configuration are the culprit. I\'m investigating how those config parameters affect the `InputManager`, and how to make the optional fields default! I\'m checking `VirtualStickParams` in `steering.ts` now, to ensure they\'re handled correctly.\n\n\n**Resolving TypeScript Conflicts**\n\nI\'m now investigating a TypeScript error in `GameScene.tsx(202,50)` related to missing properties in `GameSettings`, specifically `stickSelfCenterRate` and `maxStickDeflection`. The issue seems to stem from `InputManager.ts`, where these properties are optional in `VirtualStickParams`. I am examining how `STEERING_CONFIG` and `STEERING_BOUNDS` from `steering.ts` are used to provide defaults and ensure the correct parameters.\n\n\n**Fixing Aiming Overshoot**\n\nI\'ve traced the aiming overshoot to excessive sensitivity at the center due to a high `precisionLinearBlend` value in the steering config, causing the reticle to jump too quickly, even with small mouse movements. The function `g(u) = a * u + (1 - a) * u^p` is at fault. It appears a high `a = 0.25` even gives a small input more weight. I will explore reducing this factor to give it less weight.\n\n\n**Quantifying Steering Sensitivity**\n\nI\'m now quantifying the effect of the steering response curve, especially at low mouse input. I\'ve found an excessive yaw rate slope at the center (0.875 rad/s) and problematic rates for fine aiming. Small mouse movements (4-9 pixels/frame) cause substantial turn rates (12-57 deg/s), easily overshooting small targets (1.2-1.6 degrees). This causes a cycle of over-correction.\n\n\n**Assessing Agility Parameters**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the unrealistic steering rates. I\'ve found that `maxYawRate = 3.5 rad/s` and `maxPitchRate = 3.0 rad/s` are inflated 67% above the ship\'s designed agility. This global amplification causes over-sensitivity. My goal is to see how this affects different ship designs.\n\n\n**Tracing Gimbal Sensitivity**\n\nI\'m now investigating the gimbal limits and their interaction with mouse input. Given that 400 px/s equates to u=0.25 and a rotation rate of 24.1 deg/s, even tiny mouse movements cause a significant and visually jarring reticle response. The gimbal offset caused by `aimCursorX` and `GIMBAL_MAX_YAW_RAD` amplifies the issue. The clamped angles in `getAimDirectionWorld()` may contribute to this.\n\n\n**Examining Steering Calculations**\n\nI\'m now investigating how `normTurnX` and `normTurnY` are used to compute the desired reticle position. These normalized velocities, being clamped, drive the reticle position which in turn drives the ship\'s rotation. The clamped `normTurnX` is the cause of the reticle jumping, not the source of the mouse input.\n\n\n**Tracing Aiming Jump**\n\nI\'m now investigating the reticle\'s behavior when mouse input ceases. The `aimCursorX` variable, linked to angular velocity, results in the boresight reticle jumping ahead during mouse movement, then reversing after. Specifically, the reticle jumps +6.3 degrees ahead and BACKWARDS 6.3 degrees! The reticle\'s deflection, caused by `aimCursorX`, combined with the integration issues I\'ve already described, are now my primary focus.\n\n\n**Verifying Crosshair Motion**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the interaction between ship rotation and reticle deflection. Specifically, the crosshair moves at the rate of `shipRotation + gimbalDeflection`, leading to a significant overshoot. During micro-corrections, `aimCursorX` oscillates due to gimbal movement, causing a pendulum-like motion and 55ms lag. This contributes to the aiming problem. I will examine how the aiming jump happens.\n\n\n**Diagnosing Phase-Lead Instability**\n\nI\'ve identified a clear phase-lead instability in the aiming system. It\'s caused by the velocity signal\'s effect on positional gimbal offset. As the mouse stops, the gimbal offset vanishes. This creates a visually jarring \"snap-back,\" making aiming difficult, and resulting in an unstable control loop.\n\n\n**Evaluating Camera Lag**\n\nI\'m now investigating camera behavior, focusing on lines 71-78 in `CameraController.ts`. Specifically, I see `lagX` and `lagY` are clamped versions of `flight.yawVelocity` and `flight.pitchVelocity`, respectively, creating a camera lag effect. This lag contributes to the aiming overshoot due to the camera\'s subsequent correction. The camera snapback will also make this worse!\n\n\n**Investigating Gimbal Calculations**\n\nI\'m now investigating how the gimble parameters are used in calculations to affect `getAimDirectionWorld`. In `FlightController.ts`, I\'m looking at how the `GIMBAL_MAX_YAW_RAD` (0.22 radians) and `GIMBAL_MAX_PITCH_RAD` (0.17 radians) limits are applied. I\'m focusing on the interaction between the `steeringMode`, the gimble angles, and their resulting impact on the aiming direction calculation.\n\n\n**Fixing Mouse Control Scheme**\n\nI\'m now focused on the unintended behavior in the direct-nonlinear mouse steering mode. The ship steers with mouse input, but the boresight crosshair jumps ahead of the ship, up to 12.6 degrees, before snapping backward upon mouse cessation, leading to instability. The underlying issue is related to how `aimCursorX` is used.\n\n\n**Verifying Boresight Motion**\n\nI\'m verifying the boresight behavior to assess the crosshair snap-back. I\'m focusing on the effect of the gimbal\'s yaw on `boresightScreenX` using the enemy fighter\'s position and FOV parameters. This should quantify the snap-back\'s visual impact and ensure the issue\'s severity.\n\n\n**Quantifying Crosshair Motion**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the magnitude of the reticle jump, its relation to screen dimensions, and the impact of mouse movement and cessation. I\'m focusing on the jump, and how the `virtual-mouse-cursor` and `boresight` reticles jump. The reticles jump when a small, normalized mouse movement and cessation is inputted. I will quantify the screen jumps.\n\n\n**Revising Boresight Logic**\n\nI\'m now investigating the specific problems with `getAimDirectionWorld` in `direct-nonlinear` mode, where I found that the gimbal offset is a velocity-proportional lead offset. I\'m focusing on the effect of `GIMBAL_MAX_YAW_RAD` and how the reticle displacement should behave. The relationship between the ship and reticle, when the gimbal is zeroed, should point the reticle along the ship\'s nose.\n\n\n**Refining Crosshair Logic**\n\nI\'m now refining the logic governing the boresight reticle. My goal is to ensure that the boresight aligns directly with the target without any snap-back upon mouse cessation. I am now evaluating the interaction between `FlightHUD.tsx`, `boresightRef`, and `aimReticleRef` in `FlightController.ts`. Specifically, I\'m verifying the behavior of `boresightScreenX` and `boresightScreenY` and confirming no snap-back occurs when mouse input stops, ensuring laser convergence remains on target.\n\n\n**Analyzing Reticle Snapback**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the snap-back behavior of the reticle, specifically when `yawVelocity` goes to zero. It takes roughly 160ms for `reticleAlpha` to settle, causing the reticle to reverse its position, and creating an overshoot during mouse stops. I will analyze what causes the `yawVelocity` to zero out so quickly.\n\n\n**Improving Reticle Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now focusing on improving reticle dynamics, primarily addressing the lag and erratic movement of `joystickX`. I want this value to decay to center. Fine aiming should see a tightly centered reticle that moves smoothly. Large turns should show the turn vector clearly. I am assessing how to make `joystickX` settle correctly. I am also investigating how to ensure smooth transitions.\n\n\n**Evaluating Boresight Accuracy**\n\nI\'ve verified that setting `GIMBAL_MAX_YAW_RAD` and `GIMBAL_MAX_PITCH_RAD` to zero eliminates the jump, and stopping the mouse leaves the aiming crosshair stationary on target. This indicates the overshoot is entirely from velocity-driven gimbal offset. I\'m now re-examining the requirements and tests to see if this change violates any.\n\n\n**Refining Control Curve**\n\nI\'m now revising the control curve to meet requirements. The goal is to ensure that mouse stops stop steering, and the curve achieves precise small movements, continuous large turning, and preserve all other features. The \"no global sensitivity changes\" and the \"do not change\" rules from our prior assessment are central. I\'m focusing on the effect on `a` and `p`.\n\n\n**Evaluating Sensitivity Metrics**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on sensitivity to pinpoint the root of the overshoot. Specifically, for a small micro-nudge of just 1 pixel/frame, a current yaw rate of **2.23\302\260/s** is seen.\n\n\n**Optimizing Aiming Sensitivity**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the excessive sensitivity at low mouse input, stemming from the response curve and the `precisionLinearBlend` value. Tiny mouse movements cause over-aggressive yaw rates (12-31 deg/s), easily leading to overcorrection. The problem comes from `precisionLinearBlend = 0.25`, and a high slope at the origin.\n\n\n**Refining Input Normalization**\n\nI\'m now revising the input normalization range and response curve to address the overshoot. The current configuration prevents gentle, small movements. The formula g(u) = a * u + (1 - a) * u^p requires adjustment. I will use `precisionLinearBlend = 0.08` to reduce the center slope, and `responseCurve = 2.25` to strengthen the nonlinear progression for better fine-aiming.\n\n\n**Evaluating Sensitivity Refinements**\n\nI\'m now revising the control scheme, specifically focusing on improved fine and medium control. I\'m testing `precisionLinearBlend = 0.08`, `responseCurve = 2.25`, and `inputNormalizationRange = 1800` to refine aiming. I\'ve calculated a yaw rate of 0.46\302\260/s for a 1 px/frame movement, showing sub-pixel precision.\n\n\n**Refining Steering Parameters**\n\nI\'m now refining steering parameters, aiming for smooth, precise control. I\'ve analyzed and improved the control curve, targeting sub-pixel precision for micro-aiming, by revising `precisionLinearBlend`, `responseCurve`, and `inputNormalizationRange`. A 1 px/frame movement yields 0.46\302\260/s now. Previously, 2.23\302\260/s. I\'ve re-calculated expected yaw rates, now with significant improvements.\n\n\n**Calculating New Yaw Rates**\n\nI\'m now recalculating yaw rates with the updated parameters to confirm improvements. For 1 px/frame, the result is now 0.61\302\260/s, a huge leap from 2.23\302\260/s. At 8 px/frame, it\'s 10.18\302\260/s, medium is 34.77\302\260/s at 15 px/frame, and strong is 100.9\302\260/s at 25 px/frame. Large turn (30 px/frame) gives 143.6\302\260/s, indicating significant refinement.\n\n\n**Tracing Gimbal Coupling**\n\nI\'m now investigating Factor 2: how velocity coupling affects the weapon gimbal and boresight reticle in `FlightController.ts`. Specifically, I\'m focusing on the code where `GIMBAL_MAX_YAW_RAD` and `GIMBAL_MAX_PITCH_RAD` are defined and used within `getAimDirectionWorld()`. The calculations in direct-nonlinear mode will be central to resolving the issue.\n\n\n**Fixing Aiming Jumps**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on an issue: the ship\'s rotation causes the boresight crosshair to jump ahead and then snap backward. I\'ve found that `aimCursorX` and `aimCursorY` are driven by instantaneous ship velocity, decaying to zero when the mouse stops, making the reticle move erratically.\n\n\n**Eliminating Boresight Oscillation**\n\nI\'m now focused on the root cause: `aimCursorX`\'s sign alternation leads to boresight reticle whipping, causing oscillation. By using a zero velocity-gimbal offset in `direct-nonlinear` mode, the reticle moves with the ship\'s angular rate. This method eliminates the snap-back, providing precise aiming and stable tracking.\n\n\n**Verifying Cursor Response**\n\nI\'m now investigating the outer HUD cursor\'s response in `FlightController.ts`. Specifically, I see how `telemetry.aimScreenX` and `telemetry.aimScreenY` are influenced by `normTurnX` and `normTurnY`. Lines 470-501 reveal that `joystickX` and `joystickY` are calculated using `yawVelocity` and `pitchVelocity`, which now should work since the gimbal whiplash is gone!\n\n\n**Refining HUD Cursor Behavior**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the outer HUD cursor, specifically `joystickX` and `joystickY`, key drivers of the `virtual-mouse-cursor`. I\'m evaluating its behavior when the mouse stops or reverses, aiming for smooth turn vector indication and crisp settling to center. I see that faster settling, e.g. using `28.0` for turns and `45.0` to snap, significantly improves the visual experience.\n\n\n**Evaluating Input Smoothing**\n\nI\'m now investigating how `stepDirectAngularRate` in `MouseResponseCurve.ts` handles input smoothing, particularly at low velocities. I see that effective tau is crucial for stopping and reversing, ensuring smooth transitions and preventing abrupt changes in angular rates. This is another area of optimization I can use.\n\n\n**Evaluating Angular Deceleration**\n\nI\'m now investigating the `stepDirectAngularRate` function and how it implements user requirements. I see, when the mouse stops or reverses, unwanted angular momentum can persist, even with the user setting a high `inputSmoothing` value. Currently, it\'s not stopping steering demand!\n\n\n**Refining Mouse Deceleration**\n\nI\'m now focused on residual angular momentum after the mouse stops and how to correct for this. Specifically, if mouse movement stops, but the angular velocity remains, the ship keeps turning. I\'m also examining the direction reversal, where the angular velocity is delayed if the blending factor (`alpha`) is too low. My plan now is to reset the `currentRate` to zero upon direction change or stop, with a higher decay multiplier.\n\n\n**Refining Angular Control**\n\nI\'m now implementing new strategies to address angular momentum, aiming for immediate rotation stops and instantaneous reversal. My update to `stepDirectAngularRate` clears residual angular velocity upon direction change or cessation, ensuring precise control. This is done by setting currentRate to `0` instead of a small value, so my calculations now have improved directionality.\n\n\n**Migrating Configuration Settings**\n\nI\'m now investigating a crucial potential issue: saved local storage settings. Specifically, if a user had an older version, the application might load outdated steering parameters from `localStorage`, overriding my recent calibration, due to the existing `starfall.settings.v7` key. I plan to increment the storage key to `\'starfall.settings.v8\'` to prevent this and migrate user preferences.\n\n\n**Updating Configuration Handling**\n\nI\'m now focused on migrating user settings from `localStorage` to ensure the correct steering parameters are loaded. I\'m bumping `SETTINGS_STORAGE_KEY` to `\'starfall.settings.v8\'` to prevent older settings from overriding my recent calibration, as in `config/ships.ts`, lines 181-296. I\'m also ensuring defaults are applied to older versions. I will make sure the changes support baseline joystick configuration.\n\n\n**Fixing Steering Parameters**\n\nI\'m now tracing missing config parameters that caused errors in `InputManager.ts` and `GameScene.tsx`. It appears `consumeSnapshot()` was trying to access `stickSelfCenterRate` and `maxStickDeflection` from `STEERING_BOUNDS` and `baseMouseSensitivity` from `STEERING_CONFIG`. I\'m updating `steering.ts` to include these and setting up default values for them.\n\n\n**Resolving Input Configurations**\n\nI\'m now focused on addressing the `consumeSnapshot()` exception by restoring missing parameters to the steering configuration. Restoring `stickSelfCenterRate`, `maxStickDeflection`, and `baseMouseSensitivity` in `steering.ts` should resolve the TypeScript errors without affecting Pointer Lock. Updating `InputManager.ts` to handle optional parameters will ensure compatibility.\n\n\n**Refining Nonlinear Curve**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into the nonlinear response curve, specifically focusing on how to calibrate it to meet Section 2 and 3 requirements. I\'m prioritizing fine-aiming precision by reducing sensitivity at the center, preserving large-turn capability, and ensuring predictable transitions. I\'ll focus on how the curve affects small corrections and avoids oscillation, unwanted momentum.\n\n\n**Analyzing Nonlinear Curve**\n\nI\'m now investigating `g(u) = a * u + (1 - a) * u^p`, to understand its effects across small, medium, and large inputs. I see small inputs (< 7.5 px/frame) are crucial, with a slope of `a` at 0. Higher-order derivatives ensure smoothness, while the curve\'s end point stays at u=1, as expected.\n\n\n**Calibrating Steering Parameters**\n\nI\'m now implementing the new steering curve, aiming for precise micro-adjustments and smooth, continuous large turns, per requirements. The aim is zero distortion, with C2-continuous tanh soft saturation, with values for both, derivatives. I\'ve noted a 68% reduction in the linear center slope.\n\n\n**Optimizing Sensitivity Metrics**\n\nI\'m now implementing calibration changes to provide the steering response curve, focusing on optimizing sensitivity metrics for precision. I\'m prioritizing adjustments to `inputNormalizationRange`, `maxYawRate`, `maxPitchRate`, and `precisionLinearBlend`, along with a new value for `inputSmoothing`. These changes should significantly improve fine aiming and handling.\n\n\n**Verifying Velocity Coupling**\n\nI\'m now re-examining how `getAimDirectionWorld` functions within the velocity coupling for direct-nonlinear mode, to verify my previous assessment. Specifically, I\'m confirming that the velocity-driven gimbal offset has been eliminated. The elimination of the snap-back is the top priority!\n\n\n**Verifying Forward Vectors**\n\nI\'m now tracing the direct-nonlinear steering mode, specifically the forward vector calculations in `getAimDirectionWorld()`. When the mode is active, the ship\'s forward vector is calculated directly, ensuring the cannons converge at a defined distance along the ship\'s true forward, and that the reticle aligns accordingly.\n\n\n**Verifying Convergence Accuracy**\n\nI\'m now verifying that the boresight reticle stays rock-solid on the target upon mouse cessation in direct-nonlinear mode. Specifically, the reticle moves with ship rotation and has zero post-stop snap-back. I\'m confirming convergence accuracy and stable tracking for a better aiming experience.\n\n\n**Verifying Controller Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now verifying the current reticle behavior aligns with Section 4\'s constraints. I\'m confirming that the altered `reticleAlpha` values do not violate pointer lock, input handling, mouse-edge fixes, aim boundary, reticle reset, HUD layout, camera architecture, or combat/weapon systems. The cursor now smoothly recenters quickly.\n\n\n**Finalizing Config Parameters**\n\nI\'m now implementing the final configuration parameters and reviewing the core logic in `steering.ts`, `InputManager.ts` to solidify the latest steering curve. Specifically, I\'m integrating the revised `DIRECT_MOUSE_STEERING_CONFIG` with improved sensitivity, smoothing, and decay for the best experience. The aim is zero distortion, with C2-continuous tanh soft saturation, with values for both, derivatives. The goal is to provide a clean user experience.\n\n\n**Refining Mouse Deceleration**\n\nI\'m now implementing strategies to stop all angular momentum when the mouse stops or reverses direction. By resetting `currentRate` in `stepDirectAngularRate` to `0` during changes in direction or mouse cessation, I\'m ensuring precise control. This should greatly improve directional accuracy.\n\n\n**Implementing Steering Edits**\n\nI\'m now implementing the edits to eliminate the boresight jump and refine the reticle stop-settling, which should ensure the reticle aligns directly with the ship\'s forward vector, eliminating the snap-back. I\'m also updating the storage key to `\'starfall.settings.v8\'` to prevent old settings overriding the new parameters. I am preparing for validation tests!\n\n\n"
