  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Code Snippets**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into `FlightController.ts`, specifically lines 473-510, and also `InputManager.ts`, lines 297-325. My primary focus is on the implementation details of the Independent Aim Boundary HUD indicator. I\'m trying to wrap my head around the visual reticle\'s behavior and how it reacts to input, specifically the clamped turn demands within the rounded rectangle. I\'m keen on understanding the logic behind this interaction.\n\n\n**Deciphering Reticle Behavior**\n\nI\'m currently focusing on how the reticle self-centers when mouse input ceases. Specifically, the clamping of yaw and pitch velocities to normalize turn demands, and then calculating `desiredReticleX` and `desiredReticleY` has captured my interest. The smoothing with `reticleAlpha` to prevent jitters, using `reticleRate` as a key factor in these calculations, is intriguing. I\'m especially interested in `isReticleStopping`\'s use in these calculations.\n\n\n**Visualizing Reticle Movement**\n\nI\'m now visualizing how the reticle moves, focusing on `this.joystickX` and `this.joystickY`. I\'m exploring the behavior when the reticle is clamped to the aim boundary, particularly when we remove any recentering logic. I\'m especially interested in how it responds to continuous mouse movement at the boundary and what happens when the mouse reverses direction. I\'m focusing on the clamping effect and full steering demand.\n\n\n**Investigating Joystick Logic**\n\nI\'m now investigating what happens when the reticle is immediately moved inward, reversing steering demand. The code involving mouse sensitivity, resolution scaling, and the `ySign` to handle inverted Y-axis inputs is the focus. I\'m looking at how `clampToRoundedRect` impacts the joystick\'s `clampedX` and `clampedY`. I also need to figure out how `this.joystickEval` should be populated, so I\'m investigating how that should happen.\n\n\n**Exploring Radial Magnitude**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on `this.joystickEval.radialMagnitude`. I see in `FlightHUD.tsx` how it\'s used to update the reticle\'s dataset and influence visual elements like opacity. I\'m calculating the exact radial magnitude of the joystick position relative to the aim boundary using `computeRoundedRectRayRadius`, determining a ratio between the actual joystick distance and the boundary radius. I now understand that this `reticleRadial` is clamped to values between 0 and 1, a crucial aspect.\n\n\n**Calculating Steering Values**\n\nI\'ve been analyzing the steering-related calculations. I\'ve broken down how `normTurnX` and `normTurnY` are derived from pitch and yaw velocities, and how `steerMag` is determined. I am now understanding how `this.joystickEval` is populated with `radialMagnitude`, `steeringMagnitude`, `steerX`, `steerY`, and `zone`, with the zone determined by `reticleRadial`. I am focused on the independence and cleanliness of the physical mouse input and visual reticle position values.\n\n\n**Isolating Mouse Input**\n\nMy attention is now on how the system translates direct mouse movements into steering demands, specifically `this.directMouseEval.steerYaw` and `this.directMouseEval.steerPitch` which directly influence `targetYawRate` and `targetPitchRate`. This calculation is independent of the reticle\'s clamped position. I\'m also examining the `InputManager.ts` file, lines 302-319, and the implications of `lockOwned` in the input handling.\n\n\n"
