  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Gameplay Dynamics**\n\nI\'ve been analyzing the user\'s feedback concerning the reticle behavior in the game. It seems the forced rebound is gone, as confirmed, but a significant problem persists: the reticle behaves erratically when it meets the aim boundary. I need to consider why this is happening.\n\n\n**Investigating Ship Turning**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the ship\'s erratic turning when the reticle hits the aim boundary. Specifically, why does turning cease, requiring repetitive mouse movements? I\'m considering the interplay between human input (mouse movement), the reticle\'s behavior, and the visual cues (aim boundary, steering tether). I need to determine if there\'s a disconnect between player intention and in-game ship response. I\'m exploring the nuances of continuous versus intermittent turning with the specific interface elements in mind.\n\n\n**Re-examining Mouse Input**\n\nOkay, I\'m now zeroing in on the incredibly small mouse movements involved. I\'m visualizing the physical distance (0.12 inches!) representing the reticle\'s movement to the aim boundary, and I\'m questioning why the ship *stops* turning given such a subtle input change. I need to figure out what\'s really happening when the player holds the reticle at the boundary, which seems to prevent the problem, versus moving the mouse repeatedly. I\'m going to look at the user\'s comments.\n\n\n**Decoupling Steering Inputs**\n\nI\'m now separating mouse input, reticle position, and steering demand. I plan to read raw mouse deltas directly from Pointer Lock without discarding any data. The reticle will remain clamped but its position won\'t drive steering. Instead, I\'ll convert mouse deltas into continuous flight-control input to drive the ship. My goal is to decouple the physical mouse input from the bounded reticle position to deliver smooth, continuous steering, irrespective of the reticle\'s visual limit.\n\n\n**Isolating Steering Input**\n\nI\'m now focused on isolating steering demand from the clamped reticle position, to decouple visual reticle position from ship control. My goal is continuous steering based on mouse input. I will need to ensure smooth control and that the aiming response curve is preserved, with no artificial inertia or virtual joystick effects. I\'m going to look for any logic that may be calculated from the clamped reticle coordinates, discarded mouse deltas, input resets at the boundary, or boundary collision routines that zero steering output.\n\n\n"
    message_id: "skT_asH1Fdjqz7IPqJbGuA8"
