  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Observing Physical Input**\n\nI\'m now closely observing the real browser\'s behavior during physical mouse interactions within the game. Specifically, I\'m tracing the events occurring when a user\'s mouse moves the reticle (`virtual-mouse-cursor`) towards the aim boundary. The initial focus is on capturing the sequence of these events, step-by-step.\n\n\n**Calculating Mouse Distance**\n\nI\'ve determined the physical mouse distance required is 192 pixels to move the reticle to the aim boundary, and I\'ve estimated it takes 0.15 to 0.25 seconds. Now, I\'m delving into the turning dynamics of the ship in `FlightController.ts` within that timeframe, assuming a movement of 192 pixels in 0.3 seconds. I\'m exploring the correlation between mouse movement and the ship\'s rotational response, specifically working through the math of how `joystickX` is affected and translates into ship rotation.\n\n\n**Analyzing Rotational Discrepancy**\n\nI\'ve just crunched some numbers, and I\'m stunned by the results. Moving the mouse all the way to the aim boundary, a distance of 192 pixels, leads to an absurd rotational difference! Even a fast mouse movement, the ship barely rotates. My calculations show it only rotates around 5 degrees, even if the mouse travels to the boundary! This is with a slower speed. If the mouse is fast, it\'s only about 10 degrees. There\'s a severe disconnect between input and output. I need to re-evaluate the rotational physics.\n\n\n**Quantifying Angular Response**\n\nThe discrepancy is more pronounced than I initially calculated! Now, I am quantifying the degrees of rotation given a reticle interaction. When the reticle meets the Aim Boundary, the ship only rotates a mere 3.2 degrees! Considering the need for a 180-degree turn, the math is staggering. A rate of 10.6 px/frame requires 6,580 pixels (8.2 inches) of physical mouse movement to rotate 180 degrees. At 6 px/frame, it needs 10,080 pixels (12.6 inches)! The player will run out of space very fast!\n\n\n**Examining Prompt Hypotheses**\n\nI am now carefully examining the user\'s prompt sentence-by-sentence. My focus is on two hypotheses: why a player would move the reticle to the aim boundary, and what happens when they move the mouse continuously, versus when the reticle hits the boundary. I\'m testing both of these against every statement. The aim is to thoroughly validate the given failure conditions and the required architecture, which emphasizes separating relative mouse input from the reticle\'s position and steering demand.\n\n\n**Refining Continuous Steering**\n\nI\'m now integrating raw mouse deltas from Pointer Lock, processing each input precisely. My priority is to prevent discarding input, regardless of the reticle\'s boundary. I\'m focusing on ensuring steering demand correlates directly with continuous mouse movement, independent of the clamped reticle position. I\'m focusing on continuous rightward or leftward mouse movement to test steering response.\n\n\n**Diagnosing Boundary Interaction**\n\nI am now inspecting the code for potential causes of the ship\'s interrupted turning when the reticle hits the aim boundary. Specifically, I\'m hunting for the origin of the observed intermittent rotation. I\'m focusing on how steering is calculated from clamped reticle positions, and whether mouse deltas are discarded or reset when the reticle reaches the boundary. I\'m also examining the collision branches and any input consumption conflicts.\n\n\n**Investigating Lock Activation**\n\nI\'m now investigating a crucial aspect: the influence of Pointer Lock. I need to verify that its activation state, both active or inactive, isn\'t interrupting the ship\'s continuous turning. I\'m focusing specifically on the conditions within `InputManager.ts` that might be causing a stop in rotation. I\'m looking for the logic that ties the mouse movement handlers to Pointer Lock status to determine the flow of events during mouse movement in the browser.\n\n\n**Tracing Mouse Events**\n\nI\'m now tracing the sequence of browser events during continuous mouse movement while Pointer Lock is active. Specifically, I\'m verifying the generation of `mousemove` events with `e.movementX` and `e.movementY` data and checking how these events are handled in `InputManager.ts` to calculate `this.mouseDeltaX` and `this.mouseDeltaY`. I\'m confirming this in relation to `GameScene.tsx` and verifying the data snapshot is appropriately used in the game loop.\n\n\n**Calculating Mouse Speeds**\n\nI\'m now calculating real-world mouse speeds to estimate expected deltas over several seconds, specifically considering the `FlightController`\'s update loop. My calculations suggest a range of 1000 to 1500 pixels per second is achievable with continuous mouse movement. I\'m focusing on validating these speeds with actual in-game testing to ensure the rotational response is appropriately adjusted to match.\n\n\n**Evaluating Flight Response**\n\nI\'m now focusing on calculating how the flight controller responds to continuous mouse input at speeds of 300 CSS pixels per second. I\'ve determined it takes 0.64 seconds for the visual reticle to reach the aim boundary at this speed. Now, I\'m examining how the flight controller interprets this, to understand the ship\'s rotation speed. I\'m carefully analyzing the impact on the `joystickX` value at that input rate.\n\n\n**Calculating Yaw Rate**\n\nI\'ve just calculated the target yaw rates based on specific mouse speeds, and the results are eye-opening. For the input speeds I tested, with reticle speeds from 180-300 px/s, the ship barely rotates. A mere 1.96 degrees/s at 180 px/s! I need to re-examine the `evaluateNonlinearGain` function. The ship needs to be more responsive to mouse input, and these values are far too low.\n\n\n**Validating Angular Calculation**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the nonlinear gain calculations. My focus is on understanding why the current math results in such a slow s