the apparent disconnect: intermittent steering, in the current failure case, versus continuous steering expected in the tests. The intermittent turning is clearly the key issue that needs resolution.\n\n\n**Investigating Test Failure**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on why the user\'s acceptance tests 1, 2, and 3 failed in the browser. I\'m focusing on every potential reason for this failure, including the conditions when the reticle touches or crosses the boundary. I\'m scrutinizing every assumption made up to this point, to see what could have blocked this test from passing. Specifically, I\'m checking Reason 1, Reason 2, Reason 3, and Reason 4 for all possible failure causes.\n\n\n\n**Examining Pointer Lock Behavior**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a critical point: Pointer Lock\'s state and timing during mission launch. Specifically, I\'m analyzing the `handleStartMission` function, particularly lines 273-325 in `App.tsx`, and how Pointer Lock is requested, and when the loading phase begins. I\'m focusing on the potential for Pointer Lock failure, especially right after the \"Launch\" button is clicked, and the flow of events from that point.\n\n\n**Verifying Lock Transitions**\n\nI\'m now investigating how the game syncs `InputManager` and flight audio with the active gameplay phase. I\'m focusing on the `useEffect` hook in `App.tsx`, and how the `isPlaying` state influences `setActive`. I\'m also examining the `handlePointerLockChange` function within `InputManager.ts` to see how the active state and pointer lock interacts when the user moves their physical mouse continuously in one direction.\n\n\n\n**Analyzing Mouse Delta Updates**\n\nI\'m now investigating the relationship between browser `mousemove` events and the game\'s update loop. My focus is on understanding the frequency of `mousemove` events in various browser and display setups, including the impact of continuous mouse movement versus the game\'s update rate. I\'m considering the potential for discrepancies between mouse polling and display refresh rates, and how this impacts the accumulation of `mouseDeltaX` and `mouseDeltaY`.\n\n\n\n**Investigating Frame Discrepancies**\n\nI\'m now investigating a critical issue: frame-rate discrepancies and their effect on mouse input. I\'ve realized mouse movement events are not always delivered on every render frame. When the mouse moves slowly, events get coalesced, and `input.mouseDeltaX` equals zero. This is a problem when a single frame appears where `mouseDeltaX` is zero, because the controller will reset the `targetYawRate` back to zero, halting rotation. I\'m focusing on `MouseResponseCurve.ts` specifically, lines 158-167 and 258-273, to see how the code handles `mouseDeltaX = 0` on any given frame.\n\n\n**Fixing Unintended Rotation Stops**\n\nI\'ve discovered the root cause of the unintended rotational stops. The `stepDirectAngularRate` function in `MouseResponseCurve.ts` was immediately setting `yawVelocity` to zero on every frame where no mouse event was received, or when `movementX === 0`. Continuous mouse movement across the desk would instantly zero out `yawVelocity` for a single micro-gap, halting the rotation.\n\n\n**Calculating Mouse Responsiveness**\n\nI\'m now calculating the ship\'s rotation with the latest gain adjustment. With a smooth mouse movement of 192 pixels over 0.5 seconds, the ship rotates 3.38 degrees. I\'m focusing on validating this updated responsiveness, to see if the ship\'s rotation speed feels more natural for players. I\'m carefully comparing the math against player expectations.\n\n\n**Validating Ship Rotation**\n\nI\'m now closely calculating the actual angular response after implementing recent gain adjustments. Moving the mouse 192 pixels in 0.5 seconds results in a 3.38-degree ship rotation. I\'m focusing on verifying that this increased responsiveness translates to a more natural and satisfying feel for the player, comparing the calculated rotation against player expectations for continuous mouse movement.\n\n\n**Calculating Mouse Physics**\n\nI\'m now focusing on re-evaluating the physical limitations of continuous mouse movement over several seconds on a mousepad. Considering a typical mousepad width, I estimate a maximum continuous input speed achievable, without repeated flicks, to be between 2.5 and 3.5 inches per second, as a baseline. I\'m performing ergonomics math on a sample pad, and then doing some verification.\n\n\n**Refining Responsiveness Math**\n\nI\'m now refining the calculations, focusing on the nonlinear gain function\'s role in ship responsiveness. I\'ve re-examined the math after calculating mouse speeds. Specifically, I\'m focusing on the `inputNormalizationRange` which is now too high. I\'m focusing on why a single flick rotates much more than continuous movement. I\'m testing the impact of different `inputNormalizationRange` values and their impact on `u`, to see how responsiveness changes as a result.\n\n\n**Optimizing Angular Responsiveness**\n\nI\'m now integrating an enhanced nonlinear gain, aimed to resolve rotational disparities at lower speeds. Previously, a 300 px/s input, the ship turned at only 4.4 deg/s. Currently, I\'m focusing on the impact of various `inputNormalizationRange` values, specifically testing the responsiveness. The goal is to make the ship\'s rotation consistent with the visual perception of reticle movement.\n\n\n**Analyzing Boundary Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now focusing on two key mechanisms, starting with why slow, continuous mouse glides after reaching the aim boundary result in little to no ship rotation, while fast flicks produce intermittent bursts. I\'m focusing on the `inputScale` value with the `r` value in the `steeringZone` to check the math. I need to explain the intermittent turning when the reticle touches the boundary at `\'max\'`. I\'m going to determine which is true.\n\n\n**Refining Steering Gain**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the steering gain and nonlinear curve, seeking greater s