g a mission.\n\n\n**Analyzing Pointer Lock Events**\n\nNow, I\'m tracing mouse movement within Pointer Lock, specifically after canvas engagement.  I\'m examining how `evaluateDirectMouseSteering` transforms `mouseDeltaX` into `targetYawRate`. My focus is on the `mousemove` event handling and `MouseResponseCurve.ts` line 169-229.\n\n\n**Calculating Continuous Deltas**\n\nI\'m now calculating real-world `dx` values within a typical browser setup, factoring in continuous mouse movement over several seconds. I\'m focusing on the effect of a large `inputNormalizationRange`. I\'m verifying the input deltas across a few browser sessions, confirming typical sustained speeds for mouse deltas. I need accurate deltas!\n\n\n**Adjusting Ship Rotational Physics**\n\nI\'m revisiting the nonlinear gain calculation, now accounting for more realistic mouse speeds based on typical hand movement. I\'ve realized the previous `inputNormalizationRange` of 1800 px/s is unrealistic, and now I\'m seeing why continuous mouse glides result in nearly no ship rotation. My next focus is on achieving a response that feels natural at all movement speeds.\n\n\n**Refining Response Curve**\n\nI am now meticulously re-evaluating and refining the gain curve, especially its response to varied mouse speeds and boundary interactions, after reviewing a real-world mouse scenario. The core issue is that continuous mouse movement is resulting in low turning rates. Specifically, I\'m focusing on why a player\'s continuous rightward mouse movements at a sustainable 200\342\200\223450 px/s only produce a 2\302\260/s turn, and how to improve this.\n\n\n**Refining Mouse Input**\n\nI\'m now carefully examining the effect of intermittent `mouseDeltaX = 0` values on ship rotation, even when continuous input is intended. I see the `yawVelocity` immediately drops to zero whenever it gets a `0`, leading to intermittent turns, and I am correcting the math. This is even when the player has already reached the Aim Boundary