    captured_user_requests {
      text: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Mouse Aiming Overshoot Reduction\n\n## Confirmed Problem\n\nThere is currently NO target-lock system.\n\nThe only problem being addressed is manual mouse aiming.\n\nThe user reports severe aiming overshoot:\n\n- Small mouse movements cause too much ship rotation.\n- The reticle frequently passes over the intended enemy position.\n- Counter-corrections cause another overshoot.\n- It is difficult to stabilize the reticle on a moving enemy.\n\nThis is a flight-control precision problem.\n\n## Objective\n\nImprove fine aiming precision without sacrificing fast directional turning.\n\nTarget style: Traditional Arcade Space Combat.\n\n## 1. Inspect the Current Controller\n\nTrace the actual relationship between:\n\n- Mouse movement.\n- Reticle displacement.\n- Steering demand.\n- Angular velocity.\n- Ship orientation.\n- Camera orientation.\n\nDetermine whether overshoot comes from excessive center sensitivity, excessive angular acceleration, accumulated input, smoothing behavior, or camera response.\n\nDo not guess.\n\n## 2. Prioritize Fine-Aiming Precision\n\nThe center region must support very small, controlled corrections.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Reduce excessive response to small mouse movements.\n- Avoid abrupt response changes.\n- Make small corrections predictable.\n- Avoid oscillation during repeated left/right corrections.\n- Ensure that stopping mouse movement stops adding steering demand.\n- Avoid unwanted angular momentum.\n\n## 3. Preserve Large-Turn Capability\n\nDo not solve the problem by simply lowering global mouse sensitivity.\n\nUse an appropriate nonlinear response curve so that:\n\n- Small inputs produce precise corrections.\n- Medium inputs produce progressively stronger turning.\n- Large inputs retain strong turning capability.\n\nThe transition must remain continuous and predictable.\n\n## 4. Strict Preservation\n\nDo not modify:\n\n- Pointer Lock implementation.\n- `movementX` / `movementY` input handling.\n- Physical mouse-edge continuity fix.\n- Aim boundary dimensions or shape.\n- Reticle reset behavior.\n- HUD layout.\n- Camera architecture.\n- Combat or weapon systems.\n\nDo not introduce a virtual joystick redesign.\n\nDo not add a target-lock system.\n\n## 5. Validation\n\nTest:\n\n1. Tiny left/right corrections.\n2. Repeated small corrections around a fixed point.\n3. Slowly tracking a moving enemy.\n4. Stopping the reticle on an enemy.\n5. Rapidly reversing steering direction.\n6. Large directional turns.\n\nMeasure overshoot and settling behavior where possible.\n\nActual browser testing with a real mouse is required before claiming the handling is fixed.\n\nIf browser testing is unavailable, report that limitation clearly.\n\n## Final Report\n\nExplain:\n\n- Root cause of overshoot.\n- Exact parameters or functions changed.\n- Why the change improves fine aiming.\n- Whether large-turn capability was preserved.\n- Actual browser test results.\n\nKeep the change small and independently reversible."
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Remove Forced Aim Boundary Recenter\n\nThe latest calibration still has a critical usability problem:\n\nWhen the in-game reticle reaches the aim boundary, it immediately snaps or rebounds toward the center. This interrupts continuous steering and makes large directional turns extremely difficult.\n\n## Required Change\n\nRemove the forced reticle recentering behavior at the aim boundary.\n\nThe boundary must NOT act as a trigger for automatic recentering.\n\nImplement the following behavior:\n\n1. The reticle moves naturally with mouse input.\n2. Reaching the visual boundary must not reset its position or steering demand.\n3. The reticle may remain visually clamped at the boundary.\n4. Mouse input must continue accumulating through Pointer Lock `movementX` and `movementY`, even when the reticle is visually clamped.\n5. Continuing to move the mouse outward must continue producing steering input.\n6. Moving the mouse in the opposite direction must smoothly reduce or reverse steering demand.\n7. No forced snap, bounce, spring return, or automatic recentering while the player is actively steering.\n\n## Important Separation\n\nKeep these systems independent:\n\n- Physical mouse input.\n- Visual reticle position.\n- Steering demand.\n- Ship angular velocity.\n\nA visual boundary clamp must never discard relative mouse input.\n\nDo not use reticle coordinates as the sole source of steering input if they are clamped at the boundary.\n\n## Preserve\n\n- Current working Pointer Lock implementation.\n- `movementX` / `movementY`.\n- Current fine-aiming response curve.\n- Existing flight architecture.\n- Current camera and HUD design.\n\nDo not redesign the Flight Controller.\n\nDo not change global sensitivity or maximum turn rates in this task.\n\n## Validation\n\nTest with a real mouse in the browser:\n\n- Slowly move the reticle toward the boundary.\n- Continue moving outward after reaching the boundary.\n- Verify that ship turning continues without interruption.\n- Reverse mouse direction and verify smooth response.\n- Perform a large directional turn.\n- Stop moving the mouse and verify no unwanted drift.\n\nReport the exact recentering logic removed or modified, relevant files, and actual browser test results.\n\nDo not claim success based only on mathematical simulation."
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Critical Fix: Continuous Steering Beyond Aim Boundary\n\n## Current Failure\n\nThe forced reticle rebound has been removed, but a critical problem remains:\n\nWhen the reticle reaches the aim boundary, the ship stops turning. The player must repeatedly move the mouse to produce intermittent turning.\n\nThis means relative mouse input is still being incorrectly coupled to the bounded visual reticle position.\n\n## Required Architecture\n\nSeparate three independent values:\n\n1. `mouseDelta`: raw Pointer Lock relative mouse movement.\n2. `reticlePosition`: bounded visual HUD position.\n3. `steeringDemand`: continuous flight-control input.\n\n### 1. Raw Mouse Input\n\nRead `movementX` and `movementY` directly from Pointer Lock.\n\nProcess every input delta exactly once.\n\nDo not discard input because the reticle has reached its visual boundary.\n\n### 2. Visual Reticle\n\nThe reticle may remain clamped to its existing rounded-rectangle boundary.\n\nHowever, its clamped position must never become the sole source of steering input.\n\nDo not automatically recenter or bounce the reticle.\n\n### 3. Continuous Steering\n\nConvert raw mouse deltas into steering demand independently of the visual reticle.\n\nExpected behavior:\n\n- Continuous rightward mouse movement produces continuous rightward steering.\n- Continuous leftward mouse movement produces continuous leftward steering.\n- Reaching the visual boundary does not change steering behavior.\n- Reversing mouse direction smoothly reverses steering demand.\n- Stopping mouse movement stops adding new steering demand.\n\nPreserve the existing aiming response curve where possible.\n\nDo not introduce artificial angular inertia or a new virtual joystick.\n\n## Important Diagnostic Checks\n\nInspect specifically for:\n\n- Steering calculated from clamped reticle coordinates.\n- Mouse deltas being discarded when the reticle reaches its boundary.\n- Input accumulation being reset at the boundary.\n- Boundary collision branches that return zero steering.\n- Input being consumed by both an event handler and a frame update.\n- A mismatch between input accumulation and reticle rendering.\n\nFix the actual root cause. Do not merely increase the boundary size.\n\n## Strict Preservation\n\nDo not modify:\n\n- Pointer Lock implementation.\n- Physical mouse-edge continuity fix.\n- Existing sensitivity settings.\n- Maximum yaw/pitch rates.\n- Camera controller.\n- Combat or weapon systems.\n- HUD layout.\n\nNo full Flight Controller rewrite.\n\n## Acceptance Tests\n\n1. Hold continuous mouse movement in one direction for several seconds.\n2. Reach the reticle boundary and continue moving the mouse.\n3. Verify uninterrupted ship rotation.\n4. Reverse mouse direction without lifting or repositioning the mouse.\n5. Verify continuous steering reversal.\n6. Repeat vertically for pitch.\n7. Verify the reticle stays visually bounded without interrupting flight control.\n\nPerform real browser testing with a physical mouse.\n\nReport the exact root cause and code changes. Clearly distinguish automated simulation from actual gameplay verification."
    }
  }
}
